S3. Reconstructed population climate niches. The first two axes of a standardized PCA fitted to1,000seeded random complete-data cells of the supplied five-variable current CHELSA regional rasters define the environmental space. Panels show environmental-prevalence-corrected kernel density for(a)nine core and(b)ten peripheral populations using their verified published classes. Black circles/triangles mark population environments. Both groups use the same geographic background; colour indicates relative corrected density on a shared0–1scale. Each population receives equal weight. This new reconstruction replaces missing plotting objects; it is not an exact reproduction of historical density estimates. The regional background is an assumed accessible-area approximation.
S4. Reconstructed niche-test null distributions for Schoener'sD(left column) and Warren'sI(right column). Grey histograms represent999randomizations and orange vertical lines show observed overlap. Top row: equivalency tests randomly exchange pooled population labels, preserving group sizes, using lower-tail probabilities. Middle row: similarity tests hold the core niche fixed and shift the peripheral niche within shared background environmental availability. Bottom row: reciprocal tests hold the peripheral niche fixed. Similarity tests use higher-tail probabilities. Panel subtitles report raw plus-one p-values; all six BH-adjusted values are supplied in the numerical test table. Null randomizations are not independent biological replicates. Tests condition on the assumed regional background, two-axis projection and spatially structured19-population sample. Nonsignificant equivalency does not establish niche identity; similarity is not a gene-flow, adaptation or rescue test. Final supplementary numbering and manuscript integration remain pending.

Both S3 panels use the same display window containing every nonzero density cell from either group and all19population environments with4percent padding. The PCA and niche tests retain the full shared regional background. No observations or nonzero densities are excluded.
